{{also|completé|complété|complète}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|compleat||archaic}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*pleh₁-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|compleet|t=full, complete}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|complet}} or {{der|en|la|completus}}, past participle of {{m|la|compleō|t=I fill up, I complete}} (whence also {{m|en|complement}}, {{m|en|compliment}}), from {{m|la|com-}} + {{m|la|pleō|t=I fill, I fulfill}} (whence also {{m|en|deplete}}, {{m|en|replete}}, {{m|en|plenty}}), ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*pleh₁-|t=to fill}} ({{cog|en|full}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kəmˈpliːt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-complete.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːt|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|com|plete}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[finish]]; to make [[done]]; to reach the [[end]].
#: {{syn|en|accomplish|finish|Thesaurus:end}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''completed''' the assignment on time.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make [[whole]] or [[entire]].
#: {{syn|en|consummate|perfect|top off}}
#: {{ux|en|The last chapter '''completes''' the book nicely.}}
# {{lb|en|poker}} To [[call]] from the [[small blind]] in an [[unraised]] [[pot]].

====Usage notes====
* This is a [[catenative]] verb that takes the ''gerund (-ing)''. See [[Appendix:English catenative verbs]]

